In article <1993Nov22.142401.6993@decuk.uvo.dec.com> (John Corne) writes:
>
>|>Hello,
>|>
>|>I was wondering if anyone can answer a question I have about the
>|>CDS client side caches.
... stuff deleted...
>|>So, how do client side caches keep in step with changes to the master
>|>namespace operated by cdsd, as the cdsd cannot propogate updates to the
>|>client side caches? (eg. the server I'm looking for moves machine, the
>|>client side cache has old information and I restart my client. How is
>|>this resolved?).
>|>

From the client side, as noted in previous articles, you can set a limit on the
period of time that cached information is considered valid via the two calls:

    rpc_ns_mgmt_handle_set_exp_age - sets the time limit for retaining cached
                                     info for the passed name service handle
                                     (ie: an inquiry context).  The time limit
                                     is used only in calls to rpc_ns*next
                                     functions; eg: binding_import_next.

   rpc_ns_mgmt_set_exp_age - sets the time limit for retaining cached info for
                             the application.

If the expiration age parameter specified to either call is 0, then effectively
no information is cached; the clerk goes to the clearinghouse every time 
information is requested.  These calls have associated *_inq* calls that 
return the current expiration age.

   You can find out if the server is up on the binding you have via
the resolve_binding and is_server_listening calls.

