wright@clam.hi.com (David Wright) writes:
> One thing to be wary of if you use DCE threads, however, is that they
> were written based on the Posix P1003.4a Draft 4 proposal.  As the
> proposal is now up to draft 8, and quite a few things have changed,
> you run the risk of being significantly out of step.
> 
> A big change is that after draft 4, all blocking system and library
> calls are thread cancellation points; this was optional in draft 4 and
> DCE threads do not assume it.

This is true.  However, I wouldn't expect many programs to use thread
cancellations extensively, especially with blocking system calls.  The
DCE documentation with any release should identify which routines are
cancellation points and which are not.

I think a bigger change from draft 4 to draft 8 is in the scheduling
mechanisms (e.g. mutexes) and signal handling.  Most especially signals,
since signals are used quite extensively by many programs.  Thread
cancellations and real-time scheduling mechanisms are less well known
and will likely not be used so much (at least in the short term).

While P1003.4a Draft 8 is the official version of threads, draft 4
probably has the most implementations (due to DCE availability).  I
suspect Draft 8 will win the day, since it is the official version.
However, a number of programs are now being written to the draft 4 spec,
and these will have to be supported for a long time to come.  Should be
interesting to see how the OSF and DCE vendors eventually handle this.

-eeb






