Dwayne Jacques Fontenot (fontenot@ravl.rice.edu) wrote:
: hello,
: 
: I have a few basic questions about DCE:
: 
: 2) Can a client access multiple servers simultaneously, if so, how?
: 
Yes.  "simultaneously" could mean a couple of things, both of which
are possible.  A client can build binding handles to connect to
multiple servers, and send calls to all of them in turn.  If several
calls need to be outstanding at the same time, you can create 
separate threads for each call.

: 4) Can multiple servers exist on a network which support the same protocol,
:    and if they do, how does a client determine which one to "talk" to, or
:    can it talk to both simultaneously?

Yes.  A client can be built to talk to any "random" server with the proper
interface, or it can be built to run through the list of relevant servers
and pick the one it likes best, or to connect to all of them.
Just a matter of writing the code you need.

: 6) If a client it "talking to" server A, which goes down, and is replaced
:    by server B (which speaks the same protocol as server A) at a different
:    network location (on another machine), does the client notice the
:    change? What would need to be done to make the above scenario work?

In general, the client will receive an exception indicating that the
connection to the server is lost; the client will then have to find
another server.
In some cases, clients that use autobinding can survive this without noticing,
as the RPC runtime can catch the exception and automatically find a new
binding.

