CRARY (CRARY@VAX5.BEAR.COM) wrote:
: In <2b8voi$d8@paperboy.osf.org> rsalz@osf.org writes:
: 
: > In <33915@ursa.bear.com> CRARY@VAX5.BEAR.COM (CRARY) writes:
: > >       After wading through the DCE security service info. I can come up
: > >with one conclusion. No, OSF is not serious about security.
: > 
: > This is wrong.
: 
:         No, you are wrong... your next paragraph says it all...
: > 
: > The DCE security system works this way.  A server can make a subroutine
: > call into the DCE library and find out the client's identity and PAC.
: 
:         Even a rank amateur appl. programmer of the server code could
:   easily subvert the intent of the security manager (person).

..

: > The server can also find out how much to trust this information -- whether
: > it has been certified by the DCE privilege server, for example.  Both
: > clients and servers can specify the amount of privacy and certification
: > they wish to use.
: > 
:        Clients and servers are application programs. When was the last time 
:   anyone in the REAL world ever TRUSTED an application program to safeguard 
:   important information? Why is it so difficult to safeguard data at the 
:   system level? 

..

There are several problems with this line of argument:

1) Off hand, I'd say that if the server is sloppily coded, any security
scheme can be subverted.  If the server uses impersonation, then you
fool the server into impersonating the wrong person...

2) Database systems are application programs.  They must be built to enforce
a variety of access restrictions.  Just because somebody else wrote the code
doesn't make it special.  It only gives you a larger group to commiserate with
when bugs are found.

3) I see no reason to believe that The System will be
more secure than an application.  No theoretical reason and no empirical
evidence.  Security problems in OSs are not exactly rare.

4) DCE is "middle-ware", implemented on top of a variety of operating systems.
It is not really feasible for DCE security to be based on specific features
of the operating system kernel.

5) Access really is properly an application decision.  Only the application
knows what the relevant division of objects is, what the meaningful operations
are, and therefore only the application can really interpret the ACLs.
Throwing it to the system would require either a very cleverly flexible
system, or a restrictively inflexible set of security primitives.

