A manual page browser, TkMan offers two major advantages over xman:
hypertext links to other man pages (click on a word in the text which
corresponds to a man page, and you jump there), and better navigation
within long man pages with searches (both incremental and regular
expression) and jumps to section headers.  TkMan also offers some
convenience features, like a user-configurable list of commonly used man
pages, a one-click printout, and integration of `whatis' and `apropos'.
Further, one may highlight, as if with a yellow marker, arbitrary passages
of text in man pages and subsequently jump directly to these passages by
selecting an identifying excerpt from a pulldown menu.  Finally, TkMan
gives one control over the directory-to-menu volume mapping of man pages
with a capability similar to but superior to xman's mandesc in that rather
than forcing all who share a man directory to follow a single organization,
TkMan gives control to the individual.  In fact, one may decide he has no
use for a large set of man pages--say for instance the programmer routines
in volumes 2, 3, 4, 8--and eliminate them from his personal database.

A new version of TkMan is now available for anonymous ftp at
ftp.cs.berkeley.edu in the /ucb/mhgs directory.  You may obtain it with the
following commands:

ftp ftp.cs.berkeley.edu
cd /ucb/mhgs
binary
get tkman-1.5.tar.Z

TkMan ***requires Tcl 7.0 and Tk 3.3***, which are also available on
ftp.cs.berkeley.edu.  TkMan 1.5 will not work with Tcl 6.7 and Tk 3.2 or
earlier versions.


Among the new features are:

* simulation of yellow highlighter markup
* better filtering out of page headers and footers
* view raw text of files and pipes
* file name completion for file names
* xman mandesc-like facility, but customizable per user
  (e.g., a non-programmer can save both space and time by excluding all the
  programmer volumes with a single line in her individual startup file)
* some keyboard bindings modifiable per user
* bug fixes:  man page names with `.'s
* support for Tcl 7.0 and Tk 3.3
* support for SGI no longer a separate code path
  (Big thanks to Paul Raines for working out SGI support.)
* when trying to print and [tn]roff source isn't available,
  can try to reverse compile the man page to produce high
  quality output


Thanks are due to Paul Raines, Chris Siebenmann, and Pierre David for
their dedicated help in bug stomping.


TkMan was developed as part of the Ensemble project at the University of
California, Berkeley, under the supervision of Professors Michael A.
Harrison and Susan L. Graham.  Ensemble is an environment for the uniform
analysis and synthesis of software and multimedia documents.

This research is sponsored in part by the Advanced Research Projects Agency
under Grant number MDA972-92-J-1028.  The content of the information does
not necessarily reflect the position or the policy of the Government.  This
research is also based in part upon work supported by the National Science
Foundation under Infrastructure Grant No.  CDA-8722788.

