In article <2cgb53$efn@bbs.pnl.gov> rg240@fermi.pnl.gov (Adrian Wong) writes:
>Can someone tell what the overall differences are between the
>various thread libraries: DECthreads, pthread, CMA? 

At a very high level the difference is this - I think DECthreads is DEC's 
threads product, I am not sure.  POSIX threads (pthreads) is a draft 
specification from POSIX describing a threads model and API.  CMA is DCE's 
threads (the CMA stands for Concert Multithread Architecture, which was the
name of the threads package DEC contributed to DCE).  DCE threads is an
implementation of a version of the pthreads spec (draft 4 - they are up to 8)
with some minor API extensions, and an exception handling package.  OSF 
will migrate to the POSIX standard behavior when the pthreads draft becomes
a final spec.

>                                                      Are threads
>considered an integral part of DCE? 

Are you're fingers an integral part of your hands?? :-) :-)  Yes, they are.

>                                    To link the RPC stuff requires
>some thread library, so if you install DCE for your machine does
>it automatically come with the thread package?

As implied by the answer to the first part of your message, the answer is 
yes.  You get the source for the DCE threads package with the DCE release.
Alternatively, you can use a native pthreads implementation.

>
>Has OSF put out a reference manual for their supported threads?
>

The pthreads reference manual is part of the DCE Application Development 
Reference Guide, which is published by (I think) Prentice-Hall, and which is 
also provide in source, ASCII and Postcript format on the DCE Release tapes.

