Adrian Wong (rg240@fermi.pnl.gov) wrote:
: Can someone tell what the overall differences are between the
: various thread libraries: DECthreads, pthread, CMA? Are threads
: considered an integral part of DCE? To link the RPC stuff requires
: some thread library, so if you install DCE for your machine does
: it automatically come with the thread package?
: 
: Has OSF put out a reference manual for their supported threads?
: 
: I've been tinkering around on the DEC AXP evaluation machine, which
: is probably why I'm so confused.....

pthreads is a Posix standard-to-be.  OSF DCE specifies Posix 1003.4a draft 4
 for their threads interface.  Posix has progressed beyond draft 4, but DCE
 is what it is.

cma is Digital's proprietary threads interface.  Digital supplied cma to OSF
 as a technology to implement user-space threads on non-threaded kernels,
 so you may see it in DCE products, but DCE programs should use the pthread
 interface.

DECThreads is Digital's supported product.  It provides an implmentation of
  pthreads (draft 4), a variant on pthreads, and cma, so you can program
  to whichever API you deem most suitable.

OSF DCE requires threads, so any DCE implementation will have threads
  one way or another, and if you get the development tools you will
  have a pthreads interface.  DECthreads, not surprisingly, is the
  way that Digital supplies threads for its DCE product on Alpha AXP.

The OSF DCE Application Development Guide and Application Develeopment
reference include threads.  They are available in the complete set from
OSF, and individually from Prentice-Hall.
	DCE Application Development Guide	ISBN 0-13-643826-1
	DCE Application Development Reference	ISBN 0-13-643834-2
The DECthreads manual should give equivalent information.


