In article <2cgb53$efn@bbs.pnl.gov>, Adrian Wong <rg240@fermi.pnl.gov> wrote:
>Can someone tell what the overall differences are between the
>various thread libraries: DECthreads, pthread, CMA? Are threads
>considered an integral part of DCE? To link the RPC stuff requires
>some thread library, so if you install DCE for your machine does
>it automatically come with the thread package?

CMA (originally "Common Multithread Architecure", later called "Concert
Multithread Architecure") was DEC's first publicly available thread API
and sample thread implementation.  DECthreads is DEC's commercial implemen-
tation of threads, which evolved from the CMA effort as CMA evolved into
pthreads.  Pthreads refers to the POSIX realtime extensions which used a
base document generated by the CMA folks.  At one time, the only difference
between CMA threads and pthreads was that CMA had a cma_ in front of every
symbol name, while pthreads had a pthread_ in front of everything.
Some implementations of pthreads are really just the DEC sample
implementation of CMA with a few tweaks.

Anyway, the pthreads API is the standard, and should be used in preference
to the CMA interface.  Since they are nearly (completely?) identical, that
should be no problem.  The use of pthreads is described in the OSF DCE
Application Development Guide and in one of the POSIX 1003 documents (I
think it's 1003.4a, "Real Time Extensions to POSIX").  I'm not positive
what the approval status is on the real-time extensions.

