In article <1993Nov19.225142.13642@ctp.com> dmcco@ctp.com (David McCowan) writes:

   I am trying to configure a cell with 1 machine (HP 867) in South
   Carolina and another (HP 867) in Cambridge MA.  Unfortunately, I have
   been unsuccessful.  The configuration of the DCE server went cleanly.
   The DCE client machine bombs trying to

	   "cdscp define cached server hardy tower ncacn_ip_tcp:<ip_address>"

   The 2 machines are connected via a router.  Has anyone tried this type
   of configuration?  Any help or comments are greatly appreciated.

What specifically happens?

Normally in the case of a LAN, CDS clients and clearinghouses find each
other via a broadcast-based protocol.  Many sites don't configure their
routers to pass broadcast traffic, and therefore another way is needed to
locate clearinghouses.  The cdscp <define cached server> command tells the
local CDS client about a server which is unreachable via broadcasting.

Note that if you do a <define cached server> and the local client already
knows about the server, an error message is printed.  Nothing is actually
broken though.  To verify this you should be able to perform operations
with the remote clearinghouse.

In 1.0.3 dce_config now examines the local clerk's cache and automatically
determines if a <define cached server> is needed.

Dave

