       The main problem:
                         In the OSF/DCE implementation of the server code,
                   the application programmer is responsible for implementing
                   the necessary calls to get the Privilege Authorization
                   Certificate (PAC) and the ACL for the object the client
                   intends to use. In addition, it's the server app. code
                   that is required to make and carry out the authorization
                   decision.

                   This is a hack (probably a leftover from the Apollo days)
                   that effectively eliminates the usefulness of DCE.
                   Imagine having to go through a security review before
                   putting any new code into production? (I'd like to meet
                   the nitwit that came up with the idea - probably works
                   in a shop with three programmers and they're all trusted
                   - of course).

                   To correct this blatant disregard for the separation
                   between system and application security I'd like to 
                   see the client pass it's ID info. transparantly to the
                   underlying OS on the server side. Then when an access of
                   data is made from the server, the server takes on the
                   ownership rights of the client. If the client isn't
                   authorized (at the system level), then an access vio.
                   would occurr. This would protect the data from a 
                   programming mistake (or worse) by the app. programmer
                   and allow security auditors to check for appropriate
                   controls, instead of code review.

          I thought I'd post here (I've heard this is where the OSF/DCE
          white tower folks communicate), to see if anybody even gives
          a rats about the users who want to use DCE. If so, I'd love
          to hear the rationale for the lousy implementation of the 
          security service.

          While I'm at it, how about a management facility to invalidate
          the CDS cache? It'd sure be nice, instead of having to wait for
          the global expiration age to expire.


          Will DCE make it as a standard? Not unless OSF gets it's act
          together.

