In <33915@ursa.bear.com> CRARY@VAX5.BEAR.COM (CRARY) writes:
>       After wading through the DCE security service info. I can come up
>with one conclusion. No, OSF is not serious about security.

This is wrong.

The DCE security system works this way.  A server can make a subroutine
call into the DCE library and find out the client's identity and PAC.
The server can also find out how much to trust this information -- whether
it has been certified by the DCE privilege server, for example.  Both
clients and servers can specify the amount of privacy and certification
they wish to use.

Access decisions are made by the server.  The DCE model is to use ACL's.
DCE ACL's look very similar to Posix ACL's.  Many of the ACL entries have
identical semantics.

Your proposal is:  a client sends its local identity to the server who
"becomes" that client in order to perform the desired request.  This is
flawed for several reasons:
    -	Security people don't generally like impersonation; it makes
	accountability hard.
    -	I don't think your idea works in a multi-threaded environment
    -	A client may not have an identity (e.g., an Mac or PC client
	accessing a DCE print server)
    -	The client's concept of identity may not map into the server's
	concept (how does a Unix uid/gid map into an MVS identity?)
