In article <CFyFC0.4wC@rice.edu>, fontenot@ravl.rice.edu (Dwayne Jacques Fontenot) writes:
|> hello,
|> 
|> I have a few basic questions about DCE:
|> 
|> 1) Can a server support multiple interface protocols (client-server protos)?

If you mean, "Can a server implement multiple interfaces?" (accept RPC calls for
a number of different interfaces), then Yes.

|> 2) Can a client access multiple servers simultaneously, if so, how?

Yes.  Either by using what is known as "explicit binding" (looking up the server
address in the directory service and then using that information as a parameter
in every RPC call) or by using "automatic binding" in conjunction with a default
search "profile" in the directory.

|> 3) When a server is moved to a different network location, what changes need
|>    to be made so that clients can still find it, if any?

Generally, just change the entry in the Cell Directory Service (CDS).  It'll be
a little more tricky if it moves to another cell, however ...

|> 4) Can multiple servers exist on a network which support the same protocol,
|>    and if they do, how does a client determine which one to "talk" to, or
|>    can it talk to both simultaneously?

Yes, you can have multiple servers with the same interface.  A client can select
a specific server from the directory or have one chosen "at random" from a group
of identical interfaces.  Yes, you can talk to more than one, but you'd have to
use explicit binding.

|> 5) Can clients/servers be updated "on the fly" without having to change
|>    something in the name binding service? That is, when a new version of a
|>    client or server is brought up, is this transparent to the rest of the
|>    "network"?

Yes (but with care).  Entries in CDS are persistent.  If a server is replaced
with a new version providing the same interface the old CDS entry would still be
valid.  The difficultly comes in managing any interactions in progress when the
server gets replaced ...

Clients don't appear in CDS and can come and go at will.

|> 6) If a client it "talking to" server A, which goes down, and is replaced
|>    by server B (which speaks the same protocol as server A) at a different
|>    network location (on another machine), does the client notice the
|>    change? What would need to be done to make the above scenario work?

Using automatic binding, and with certain restrictions on the semantics of the
RPC operations being performed (the server really needs to be stateless and
operations "idempotent") such changes can be made transparent.

|> 7) Does DCE specify a mechanism like inetd for starting servers remotely?

Not as far as I am aware.  You could build something like this for specific
services, but it would be difficult to make it as general as inetd (although ANSA
folks may have done it.)  Also, although not quite the same issue, bear in mind
that you can have a server start a new thread (up to a set maximum) to handle
each incoming RPC.  This is somewhat akin to inetd albeit on a different scale.

|> 8) Does DCE work well over long network distances (big lag times)?

That depends on what you mean by well and how much data you are shifting about in
the RPC calls.  Sorry, it's an unanswerable question without more context
(although we have run DCE over a 9.6k SLIP link, so it does at least work!)

|> That's probably enough for now. As you can tell, I am not too far into
|> DCE yet. I am just trying to see if it will do what I need.

It'll probably do what you need ... it's just a question of how well and how much
effort it will take :-) :-)

|> thanks,
|> 
|> Dwayne Fontenot

