In <2bam2c$gnl@zaphod.axion.bt.co.uk> nwinton@axion.bt.co.uk (Neil Winton) writes has already answered many of these questions.  Let me just add a few
clarifications.

>1) Can a server support multiple interface protocols (client-server protos)?

A server can support multiple interaces (e.g., set of RPC operations).
DCE also works over multiple communication protocols (tcp, ip, sna, ipx,
etc).  The comm protocols are pretty much hidden from the user.

>5) Can clients/servers be updated "on the fly" without having to change
>   something in the name binding service? That is, when a new version of a
>   client or server is brought up, is this transparent to the rest of the
>   "network"?

Yes.  In general, when a server goes down it should remove its namespace
entries (so that clients don't waste time trying to talk to something
that isn't there).  When it comes up, it re-registers itself in the
namespace and clients can find it.  (Clients read the namespace, they
never need to write to it.)  DCE RPC interfaces include major and minor
versions, and there are rules for determining which version of an RPC
interface a client connects to.  Basically, if you add a new operation
to the end of an interface, you bump the minor version and old clients can
still talk to you.  If you change an operation's calling signature, you
bump the major interface and either support both versions or old clients
lose out.

>7) Does DCE specify a mechanism like inetd for starting servers remotely?

Not currently.  We are planning on doing this for DCE 1.1.  OSF current
plans to release DCE 1.1 in the third quarter of next year.
	/r$


