We've been trying to get the DCE RPC communications component of our
system working for a couple of months now, with little success.  We've
gotten communication to work on 3 individual machines talking to themselves
using string binding to the local host (by host address, not using the
local transport.)  However, whenever we try to send data from one kind of
machine to another, the program fails.  When we send string data from VAX VMS
to HP/UX, the client core dumps.  When we send floating point data from
HP to Alpha OSF, it comes out as all zeroes.  We've verified that the HP
can talk to itself using our program and that the Alpha can talk to itself
(I think we tested VMS to itself, but I can't recall for certain.)  We haven't
done any other heterogeneous tests, since the first two failed so badly.

The fact that our program works for local communications leads us to
suspect we may be hitting interoperability bugs in RPC itself.  The problems
we've encountered are so severe, however, that we find it hard to believe
HP or DEC would have shipped DCE RPC with such problems, so we also suspect
we are doing something wrong.  We are pretty much stuck at this point.

Where should we go from here?  All the DCE training we've seen has been
much broader than we want.  We're interested in how to program using DCE
RPC, and don't much care about the rest of the stuff the DCE courses we've
seen include, so we've been working primarily from the books and occasional
help from the net.  Should we hire a consultant with strong DCE RPC skills
to examine our code?  Are there even such beasts in existence?  Have we just
encountered real live interoperability bugs which will one day be fixed?

We need to get this issue resolved.  I'd really appreciate any help or
suggestions we can get.

Thanks,
- Brad
