In article <1993Nov5.085523@cs.utwente.nl>, kremer@cs.utwente.nl (Harro Kremer) writes:
|> The documentation of DCE "Introduction to DCE 1.0" by OSF lists in a glossary
|> the
|> following terms related to RPC:
|> - at-most-once semantics
|> - idempotent semantics
|> - broadcast semantics
|> - maybe semantics.
|> 
|> It is not stated which of the semantics is applicable for the RPC that is
|> offered
|> by DCE. Does anyone know what the semantics are for DCE RPC?

All of them!  The default is at-most-once but you can declare a interface
function within its IDL definition to have any of the other semantics (although
from what I remember, broadcast is not recommended and is only there for
specialised purposes like the name service booting up).  (Of course, you have to
make sure the server implements the semantics but that's another issue ...)

|> BTW: not in the list, but other well-known semantics for RPC are
|> - at-least-once semantics
|> - exactly-once semantics.

No, DCE doesn't do these (on its own) because they imply guaranteed delivery. 
What you need is transactional semantics.  The Encina Transaction Processing
Toolkit from Transarc gives you this built on a DCE base as does CICS/6000 from
IBM (which is an implementation of CICS based on Encina and hence on DCE).

	Regards,
		Neil

PS: Of course, there really is no such thing as completely guaranteed delivery. 
A reliable queuing mechanism is the best you'll get.

