> - at-most-once semantics
> - idempotent semantics
> - broadcast semantics
> - maybe semantics.

The DCE taxonomy is slightly unconventional (at least compared
to the conventions I'm accustomed to) in that broadcast and
maybe are considered subclasses of idempotent.  In a DCE
interface definition, the default is at-most-once.  One may
specify one of idempotent, broadcast or maybe semantics for an
operation.  Idempotent implies that the operation will be
retried until it succeeds (but see below) and may be executed
more than once; maybe means the request is sent and may or may
not have been executed; for broadcast, the request is brodcast
to a set of servers and the first response received is returned
to the caller.

> - at-least-once semantics
> - exactly-once semantics.

This pair of semantics require hardware that is guaranteed to
maintain state between system crashes (at the client for
at-least-once, and both client and server for exactly-once). 
Currently, DCE makes no such assumptions and does not support
these.

-Arthur


